The first thing you should know about the Merino Sleeping Bag is that the Merino wool lining, as well as the Merino wool filler, makes it very warm and dry in extremely damp and chilly conditions, even in temperatures as low as -12°C/10°F and as high as +10°C/50°F! This is why all of Merino's sleeping bags are made with the Merino wool filling and lining. Additionally, Merino Wool is a lightweight, highly durable and breathable filling material. Its lightweight and durable qualities allow Merinus sleeping bags to provide warmth even in the harshest conditions and keep the sleeping bag feeling clean, dry, and toasty all throughout your adventure. To make sure that you enjoy a comfy, dry and warm sleep no matter what, make sure you choose the Merino Sleeping Bag. If you plan to go backcountry then you need to make sure that you get the most out of your sleeping bag, which means you need a Merino sleeping bag filled with Merino wool. Merino wool is the best filling material available for use in sleeping bags. Merino wool is the only material that can really keep you warm when the temperature drops below freezing, even when that temperature has been below freezing all night! It also absorbs a quarter of its weight in moisture and dries out to its normal weight very fast. The Merino wool in our line is fully certified as being environmentally responsible. We only buy wool that is either 100% organic Merino wool or wool that has been certified as 100% organic. We do not buy wool that has been treated with formaldehyde or any other chemicals. This means that our Merino wool sleeping bags are free of all of the chemicals that can cause skin irritations. It is important for you to know that your sleeping bag is made with 100% organic wool. This is because 100% organic wool is the only wool that is truly free of chemicals. It is important for you to choose a sleeping bag that has been filled with organic wool. It also means that your bed should not make any more noise than necessary so that you don't wake up your partner. Choose a merino fill if you want the best warmth out of your sleeping bag
